<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/11th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle on the Nemiga River</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1067</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle on the Nemiga River was fought on 3 March 1067 near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minsk">Minsk</a> between Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vseslav-of-polotsk">Vseslav of Polotsk</a> and the three sons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaroslav-the-wise">Yaroslav the Wise</a>, Iziaslav, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sviatoslav-i-of-kiev">Sviatoslav</a> and Vsevolod. The Yaroslavichi, who had sacked Minsk, defeated Vseslav and later seized him by treachery. The battle, the first event in which Minsk is recorded, is described in the Tale of Igor's Campaign.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-on-the-nemiga-river.jpg" alt="Battle on the Nemiga River" width="320" height="164" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_on_Nemiga_02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>At the end of the tenth century Prince Vladimir Svyatoslavich, then ruling <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veliky-novgorod">Novgorod the Great</a>, attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polotsk">Polotsk</a> after its prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rogvolod">Rogvolod</a> refused him his daughter. He killed Rogvolod, took the daughter, Rogneda, by force and placed his son Izyaslav in Polotsk. Izyaslav's son Bryachislav of Polotsk, who succeeded in 1001, ransacked Novgorod in 1021 but was overtaken on the Sudoma River by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaroslav-the-wise">Yaroslav the Wise</a>, defeated and forced to make peace the following year.</p><p>Bryachislav's son Vseslav succeeded him in 1044, and his campaigns in the north were more serious than his father's. He besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pskov">Pskov</a> without success in 1065, and the next year drove out the young prince Mstislav, son of Grand Prince Izyaslav Yaroslavich of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a>, and pillaged Novgorod again. The seizure threatened the ties of the Middle Dnieper princes to the north, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a>, the Baltic and northern tribute, and the dominance of the Yaroslavichi.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Iziaslav, Vsevolod and Sviatoslav marched north through the winter of 1067 and burned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minsk">Minsk</a>, then held by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polotsk">Polotsk</a>; this first mention of the town in the chronicles is marked as the date of its founding. On 3 March they came upon Vseslav's army in deep snow on the Niamiha River and defeated it. The precise course of the fighting is unknown, but it is remembered as a bloodbath, and the Tale of Igor's Campaign speaks of the bloody banks of the Nemiga sown not with blessings but with bones.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Vseslav fled back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polotsk">Polotsk</a>, and the brothers did not pursue him. In June they called for negotiations, kissed the cross in an oath and promised him safety, but when Vseslav came to Iziaslav's camp to celebrate the peace he was arrested together with two of his sons and taken to prison in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle on the Nemiga River” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Minsk first appears in the chronicle in 1067, when the sons of Yaroslav the Wise sacked it during their war with Vseslav of Polotsk and defeated him on the <b>Nemiga</b> river.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-minsk">Principality of Minsk</a></li></ul></section>
